{"schema":"reviewgraphen.benchmark.candidate_output.v1","trial_id":"m7-real:snapshot-04:full_review_graphen:1","outcome":"structured","findings":[{"local_id":"builtin-id-type-backfill-divergence","locations":[{"path":"rust/fsl-core/src/domain.rs","start_line":178,"end_line":211},{"path":"rust/fsl-core/src/domain_lowering.rs","start_line":312,"end_line":349}],"mechanism_tags":["cross_file_contract"],"rationale":"The rendering Context backfills every undeclared aggregate id_type as an external type, including built-ins Int and Bool, whereas Resolver explicitly excludes those built-ins. A domain using a built-in ID can therefore acquire a synthetic 0..1 external declaration in the text-rendering path while structured lowering retains the built-in type, producing divergent semantics or a conflicting declaration.","severity":"medium"}],"obligation_results":[{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:002fc094c6e005f602715fa93b0f61c4fae36bae6c583dadc5b585d5f5af80f1","disposition":"inconclusive","finding_local_ids":[]},{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:7fdd9390fb98dcfd18ebcc3b9665767d090038737dfc259425d88318cf82c9ed","disposition":"inconclusive","finding_local_ids":[]},{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:8471e88dfae71ba7612d85d8465cedcdee8239f3b90724284d296913c098dd24","disposition":"inconclusive","finding_local_ids":[]},{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:a74a2cc70640674782a95e5f1c06bdfad40599596b3cbab0acea8ebb2d6b2a04","disposition":"inconclusive","finding_local_ids":[]},{"packet_id":"context-envelope:sha256:eb64bb1290274de48f736736c38c0f7ffcf46ff2bfff303f5834de8ae400c8b8","disposition":"issue_present","finding_local_ids":["builtin-id-type-backfill-divergence"]}]}